

**17. Elliptic Integrals**

**Mathematical Properties**

If _R(x, y)_ is a rational function of \(x\) and \(y\), where \(y\)2 is equal to a cubic or quartic polynomial in \(x\), the integral

\[{\int}R(x,y)dx\]

is called an _elliptic integral_.

The elliptic integral just defined can not, in general, be expressed in terms of elementary functions.

Exceptions to this are

When _R(x, y)_ contains no odd powers of \(y\).

when the polynomial \(y\)2 has a repeated factor.

We therefore exclude these cases.

